          <div class="desc">SSD type required — avoid eMMC entirely</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <h2>Under 00: The Smart Choices</h2>
      <div class="callout">
        <span class="callout-label">Best Under 00 — Lenovo IdeaPad Slim 5 (~99)</span>AMD Ryzen 7, 16GB DDR5, 1TB PCIe Gen4 SSD. This is the student laptop recommendation for anyone without strong platform preferences. Battery life is solid, multitasking is smooth, and the 1TB drive handles a full academic year of project files without panic. The build is plastic but the internals are genuine. Skip any configuration with less than 16GB RAM.
      </div>
      <div class="callout">
        <span class="callout-label">Best Chromebook — Lenovo Chromebook Plus 14 (49)</span>If your coursework lives in Google Workspace and your university's LMS runs in a browser, the Chromebook Plus 14 offers 16–19 hours of battery life and a stunning OLED display for 49. The fanless design is silent in lectures. For students who don't need Windows-specific software, this is outstanding value for the display and battery combination alone.
      </div>
      <h2>00–,200: The Sweet Spot</h2>
      <div class="callout">
        <span class="callout-label">Best Windows Mid-Range — Lenovo Yoga 9i 2-in-1 Aura (~,100)</span>PCWorld's best overall laptop of 2026. Intel Core Ultra 7, 32GB RAM, 1TB SSD, 14-inch 2880×1800 OLED touchscreen at 120Hz, stylus included, 23-hour battery. The 2-in-1 form factor is particularly useful for students — tablet mode for lecture note-taking with the stylus, laptop mode for writing assignments. The display quality at this price is exceptional.
      </div>
      <div class="callout">
        <span class="callout-label">Best for Repairability — Framework Laptop 13 (~,099)</span>For engineering, computer science, or tech students who want to understand and control their hardware, the Framework 13 with AMD Ryzen AI 300 is the choice. Fully user-upgradeable. Modular ports. The lesson of the RAM shortage is that being able to upgrade your own hardware matters — Framework makes that possible.
      </div>
      <h2>,500+: If Your Parents Are Paying</h2>
      <div class="callout">
        <span class="callout-label">Best Premium — MacBook Pro 14-inch M5 (from ,999)</span>For STEM students running simulations, creative students doing video and audio production, or anyone in a Mac-dominant creative program, the M5 MacBook Pro 14 is the most powerful laptop per watt ever made. Up to 22 hours battery. Thunderbolt 5. 12MP camera that actually makes video calls look good. The price is real, but the longevity is also real — this laptop will not need replacement for 5+ years.
      </div>
      <div class="callout">
        <span class="callout-label">Best for Design Students — ASUS ProArt P16 (~,999)</span>AMD Ryzen AI processor, RTX 50-series graphics, 16-inch 120Hz OLED. For architecture, industrial design, 3D modelling, or video production students who need GPU headroom the MacBook Pro can't provide, the ProArt P16 offers Windows flexibility, upgradeable storage, and genuine discrete GPU performance. Battery life is shorter than the MacBook, and the power brick is large. For the workload, the trade-off is worth it.
      </div>
      <h2>What Not to Buy</h2>
      <p>Avoid: any laptop with <strong>8GB soldered RAM</strong> — this is the single biggest mistake students make in 2026. Avoid: <strong>eMMC storage</strong> — it will make any laptop feel slow within months. Avoid: gaming laptops as daily drivers unless gaming is genuinely your primary use case — the battery life (4–6 hours max) and fan noise make them miserable in lecture halls. Avoid: the cheapest Chromebooks under 00 — the OLED Lenovo at 49 is worth the premium for the display and longevity. </p>
